<p>
  Manufacturing turns components you keep in stock into finished products. You describe each
  product's recipe once, then plan production orders, record what's produced, and Zippiker moves
  the stock and costs for you.
</p>

<h2>1. Work centers</h2>
<p>
  Under <.link navigate="/manufacturing/work-centers">Work centers</.link>, add the places where work
  happens (Assembly, Cutting, Paint shop) with their <strong>hourly rate</strong>: what an hour there
  costs in labour and overhead. Operations done there add that cost to what you make.
</p>

<h2>2. Bills of materials</h2>
<p>
  A <.link navigate="/manufacturing/boms">bill of materials</.link>
  says what a product is made of. Choose the finished product (it must be a stock-tracked product),
  how many units the recipe makes, then add:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Components</strong>: stock-tracked products and the quantity used per batch.</li>
  <li>
    <strong>Operations</strong>
    (optional): what's done, at which work center, and how many minutes it takes per batch. Add
    instructions to print them on the job card.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  Each bill of materials shows an estimated cost per unit. A product can have several versions;
  production orders use the latest active one unless you choose another.
</p>

<h2>3. Production orders</h2>
<ol>
  <li>
    Click <strong>+ Production order</strong>
    under <.link navigate="/manufacturing/orders">Production orders</.link>
    (or <strong>Produce</strong>
    on a bill of materials), choose the product and quantity, start and due dates.
  </li>
  <li>
    Open the order to see the components needed for the rest of the run, what's in stock and any <strong>shortages</strong>, plus the estimated cost.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Confirm</strong>
    it to release it to the shop floor, and print the <strong>Job card</strong>.
  </li>
  <li>
    Each time units are finished, click <strong>Record output</strong>. You can produce in several
    runs; the order shows its progress and closes itself when the planned quantity is reached. Close
    it early if the rest won't be made.
  </li>
</ol>
<.callout kind={:warning}>
  Output can't be recorded if a component doesn't have enough stock. Receive more on a purchase
  order, or correct the count with a stock adjustment, then try again.
</.callout>

<h2>Stock and the books</h2>
<p>
  Each recorded run is a <em>Production</em>
  stock movement (<code>PRD-…</code>, under <.link navigate="/inventory/operations">Inventory → Movements</.link>): components go out at their
  average cost and the finished goods come in at components + labour/overhead per unit, which
  updates the product's average cost. In the books, inventory goes up by the labour and overhead
  added, credited to <em>Manufacturing overhead applied</em>.
</p>
